The attached image, Figure 4 from the JCR 2015 paper, illustrates the uptake of AF647-labeled Nano-11 nanoparticles (red) and FITC-labeled ovalbumin (OVA, green) by bone marrow-derived dendritic cells (BMDCs), with nuclei stained blue (DAPI) and actin white (phalloidin). The top row shows BMDCs incubated with Nano-11 alone, demonstrating nanoparticle uptake. The middle row shows minimal uptake of soluble OVA. The bottom row, showing cells incubated with OVA adsorbed to Nano-11, reveals significantly enhanced OVA uptake and clear colocalization of OVA (green) with Nano-11 (red) within the dendritic cells, indicating efficient antigen delivery by the nanoparticles. This demonstrates how Nano-11 enhances antigen delivery, a critical step for vaccine adjuvants.

The image significantly contributes to vaccine development by visually confirming that Nano-11 nanoparticles efficiently deliver protein antigens (like OVA) into dendritic cells. This direct evidence of enhanced antigen uptake and colocalization within APCs underscores Nano-11's potential as a highly effective vaccine adjuvant. This mechanism can lead to improved antigen presentation, stronger immune responses, and potentially enable lower antigen doses, making vaccine development more efficient and cost-effective, particularly for subunit vaccines that typically require adjuvants for potency.